{"kind":"assert","text":"Three rulings.\n\nA. Security group (b07e591c, 4a87cd5e, 750462d4). The native follow-up is a diagnosis and I accept it as that. It is not a gate END: no head lands on a composition of failed gates and separate stages, and no request condition waives the gate. After the native run, run one full gate at exact head 44f2a0b3 and file its END. The CLI project-creation test failed twice at about 5.4 s inside the plain stage and passes alone in 3.8 s. Before that gate, state the cause or measure it: run the plain stage alone at 94518da2 and at main 95ec0456 and give that test's time in each. If the security lanes slow it, that is a finding about the lanes. If main shows the same time, the deadline is too tight for the stage and the fix is a separate one-line head with that measurement.\n\nB. PB-1 (report 808175b7). Direction approved with these decisions.\n1. One owned live child per board, a row per member and tab, enter, update, leave and timed expiry: yes. Identity through Pinboard's existing invitation and device enrollment: yes. The unsigned name stays local until the tab has a member.\n2. Existing boards: a new install, no migration.\n3. Lease 45 seconds, renewal every 15 seconds. Pinboard expires a closed tab in 30 seconds today, and 120 seconds is a visible regression. Viewport changes are debounced at 500 ms.\n4. No read before an act and no read after the tab's own change: the watch gives the row's ID, generation and revision. Scheduled traffic is then 240 renewals per open tab per hour plus the debounced moves.\n5. The B4 binding stays as it is. Pinboard is a direct client like the map and declares no B4 view, so finding three is not a gap.\n6. Findings one and two become dap requests, filed next: the screen gateway allows the live read, live act and live stream routes; the client package gains the typed live watch and the session-aware lifecycle (read, renew, re-enter after a generation change, teardown) that the map implements in its own code. The map and Pinboard both use it.\nPinboard source waits for those two heads.\n\nC. H2 (3e606a59). The sequenced note is the ruling: section 5.4 and decision 20 say a prepare reserves its commit or abandon and the settlement of the pending work its effects open, and an allocation reserves its outcome; the build table gives H2 `fits` and `owed` in every capacity dimension. So H2 derives the terms from the selected definition branch and the prepare draft, in derive, and takes no envelope from its caller: a caller-supplied bound would move a kernel judgment into H4. Exercising both admitted wrapper forms in the pure fold, with no wider version 1 admission, is approved. Distinguishing a verified prepare source in the delivery judgment (`source-not-effective`) is H2 by the note's reason table. Where the note does not name a retained-byte term, list the term and its formula in a propose act before source; the checker reviews H2 as a kernel change under docs/kernel.md section 7. H2 keeps its place behind the security group, E2, E3 and CJ-1 for the gate.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}